abstract:       Ground-source geothermal systems using energy piles have significant potential for sustainable indoor thermal comfort. However, in cooling-dominated tropical regions, continuous heat rejection into the subsurface leads to long-term thermal accumulation and deterioration of heat pump performance, limiting the practical feasibility of conventional ground-coupled systems. This study proposes seawater-driven thermal pile battery concept for sustainable cooling in tropical coastal regions, where seawater is utilized as an auxiliary cooling source to periodically regulate subsurface thermal conditions. The surrounding soil is treated as a temporary thermal storage medium undergoing repeated thermal charge-discharge cycles during system operation. A dynamically coupled simulation framework integrating building cooling loads, seawater-assisted thermal regulation, heat pump operation, and transient ground heat transfer was developed. 
